  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: If, after the tenancy has begun, the Landlord and the Tenant agree to end it on a date they both choose, neither of them needs to give a notice of termination.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:11:23.868523Z",
 "text": "This clause is enforceable because it aligns directly with the rules governing mutual agreements to terminate a tenancy under the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*. \n\nUnder **section 37** of the Act, a tenancy may only be terminated in accordance with the Act. Specifically, subsection 37(3) provides that a notice of termination need not be given if a landlord and a tenant have agreed to terminate a tenancy. Furthermore, subsection 37(4) states that such an agreement is generally void if it is entered into at the time the tenancy agreement is made or as a condition of entering into the tenancy agreement. Because the lease clause explicitly applies only to a mutual agreement made *after* the tenancy has begun, it complies with both subsections and does not conflict with the Act.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 37",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
